course: everyday
lesson: 93
title: Boredom, excitement and pride
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Boredom is the restless feeling of having nothing to do; it is not a disaster but a chance, because boredom is often where new ideas, games and stories begin.
explain: Excitement is a happy, fizzy feeling full of energy; it is great, but it can keep you awake, make you shout indoors or make you interrupt, so it helps to know how to calm it down.
explain: Pride is the warm feeling when you have done something well; being proud of your effort is good, but boasting about what you have makes others feel small.
explain: Big moments often bring two feelings at once, such as excited and nervous before a first day, and that is completely normal.
explain: A girl in Vancouver is bored on a rainy Saturday with no screens; she builds a blanket den, reads a comic inside it, and by lunchtime she has written a story about a dragon who lives in a den.
fact: Boredom is often the start of creativity.
fact: On a rainy day you can build a den, read, draw, write a story, do a puzzle or help cook.
fact: Games like I spy, counting number plates and audiobooks make a long car journey pass faster.
fact: Learning to fill your own free time is a skill; a list of things you like doing helps.
fact: If a lesson is too easy, you can politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge.
fact: When you are too excited to sleep, reading and slow breathing help, and the day comes quicker if you sleep.
fact: Running and shouting indoors can disturb others and cause accidents, so let energy out outside.
fact: Waiting for a pause before you speak shows people respect.
fact: Being proud of your effort encourages you to keep improving.
fact: Boasting makes others feel small and pushes friends away.
fact: Having two feelings at once, like excited and nervous, is normal.
fact: A feelings diary helps you notice what makes you happy or upset.
exercise: It is raining and there are no screens. Which is a good idea? | Build a den, read, draw or help cook | Complain until a parent gives you a screen | Sit by the window and wait for the rain to stop | Go to bed early until the weather is better
exercise: Why can boredom be a good thing? | It is often the start of new ideas and games | It means your brain has switched off | It makes the time go faster | It shows you need a new toy
exercise: Which helps on a long car journey? | Playing I spy or listening to an audiobook | Asking are we there yet every minute | Kicking the seat in front of you to help pass the time | Unbuckling to stretch out on the seat
exercise: Leo says «I'm bored» to his dad again and again. What is a better plan? | Make a list of things he likes and choose from it | Wait for his dad to think of a game | Keep saying it until his dad gives in | Sit and do nothing until dinner is ready
exercise: Why is it good to learn to fill your own free time? | It is a useful skill, and parents cannot always entertain you | Because parents are not allowed to play games | Because being bored is a punishment | Because games are only fun alone
exercise: Ana finishes her maths quickly and is bored. What can she do? | Politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge | Chat loudly to the children who are still working | Do the work again more slowly next time | Put her head down and sleep at her desk
exercise: It is the night before your birthday and you are too excited to sleep. What helps? | Read something calm and breathe slowly | Jump on the bed for a long time to tire yourself out | Play a fast video game until late | Go and wake your parents for a chat
exercise: Why does getting to sleep help an exciting day come sooner? | Time passes fast while you sleep | Sleep makes all the clocks go faster | Your birthday cannot start while you are awake | Dreams make the presents arrive earlier
exercise: Jake is so excited that he runs around the flat shouting. What should he do? | Go outside to let the energy out or take deep breaths | Keep running, because being excited is good | Shout louder so everyone joins in | Jump on the sofa instead of the floor
exercise: Why is running around indoors a bad idea? | Others may be resting, and it can cause accidents | Floors indoors are always slippery and wet | Running is only allowed at school | Running indoors makes you tired too fast
exercise: Mia is excited and keeps interrupting at dinner. What is better? | Wait for a pause before she speaks | Speak louder so her news is heard first | Keep quiet and say nothing all evening | Leave the table and tell her news later
exercise: Sam worked hard on a drawing and feels proud. What should he do? | Enjoy the feeling and show it to someone | Hide it so nobody thinks he is showing off | Throw it away because it is not quite perfect | Tell everyone it is far better than theirs
exercise: A boy boasts that he has more toys than anyone. What would be better? | Share the fun of his toys instead of boasting | Boast more so that everyone admires him | Hide his toys so nobody sees them | Only play with children who have fewer toys than he does
exercise: Before her first day at a new club, Ella feels excited and nervous at once. What is true? | Mixed feelings are normal at big moments | She must choose to feel only one feeling at a time | Feeling nervous means she should not go at all | Feeling two things means something is wrong
exercise: What is a feelings diary for? | Writing or drawing how you felt each day | Listing the people who made you angry | Keeping a secret record of other people | Writing down all the rules about which feelings are allowed
test: A boy in Leeds has nothing to do on a wet afternoon. Which plan is most creative? | Write and draw his own comic | Sit at the window and wait for the sun | Ask his mum what to do every ten minutes | Lie on the sofa and complain all day
test: Which is true about boredom? | Learning to deal with it is a useful skill | It is always someone else's job to fix it | It means something is wrong with you | It only happens to children without toys
test: Ruby keeps chatting because the lesson is too easy for her. What is the best solution? | Ask the teacher politely if there is extra work | Keep chatting quietly so the others in the class do not notice | Pretend to work while she reads a comic | Tell the teacher the lesson is boring
test: The night before a holiday, Noah cannot sleep from excitement. Which helps most? | Reading a calm book and breathing slowly | Running laps of the bedroom | Looking at holiday pictures on a tablet in bed | Eating a sweet snack to relax
test: Why do people like it when you wait for a pause before speaking? | They feel respected because they can finish | It makes your news sound more exciting | It shows you are not interested in them | It gives you time to forget what you wanted to say
test: Which shows pride in a good way? | I worked really hard on this model and I'm pleased with it | My model is the best one in the whole school | Everyone else's models are rubbish compared to the one I made | I have the most expensive model kit of anyone
test: What happens when someone keeps boasting? | Others feel small and may not want to play with them | Everyone admires them more and more | They get more friends and invitations | People want to buy the same things
test: A girl in Auckland feels happy and sad at once on her last day of primary school. What is true? | Big moments often bring more than one feeling | She must be confused about how she really feels inside | One of the feelings must be fake | Happy and sad cannot be felt together
test: How can a feelings diary help you? | It shows patterns in what makes you happy or upset | It stops you from having bad feelings | It tells other people how they should be feeling each day | It replaces talking to anyone about feelings
test: so bored, raining AGAIN, nothin to do | Build a den, draw, read or help cook | Nothing to do but sit and wait | Run and shout round the house till it stops | Moan until someone fixes it
test: car trip 5 hrs to nan's, kill me now lol | Play I spy, count number plates or listen to audiobooks | Kick the seat to pass the time | Ask "are we there yet" every single minute till u get there | Nothing helps, just suffer through
test: im the best at maths in the whole class, better than all of u | Boasting makes others feel small and pushes friends away | Saying it often makes everyone like you more | It is fine because it is true | It helps others work harder
test: excited n scared at the same time for camp, is that weird | No, two feelings at once is normal | Yes, you can only feel one thing at a time | Yes, it means you should not go | Yes, it means you are ill
explain: A boy in Ohio was bored at home and climbed onto the garage roof for fun; he slipped off and broke his arm.
explain: A girl in Leeds was so excited at her party that she ran around the pool edge; she slipped on the wet tiles and needed stitches in her chin.
fact: Many childhood injuries happen when children are bored and try risky things to pass the time.
fact: When you are very excited, it is easy to stop noticing dangers like wet floors, roads and water.
test: soooo bored, gonna climb on the shed roof | Find something safe to do, falls from roofs break bones | Climb it, boredom is way worse than a broken arm | Jump off for extra fun | Only climb if no one sees
test: party at the pool, running round the edge is the best bit | Walk, wet tiles are slippery and you can fall hard | Run faster so you don't slip | Run barefoot, it grips better | Running is fine if you're excited, everyone does it at parties
test: so proud of my drawing i keep telling everyone im better than them | Be proud but don't put others down, it hurts friends | Keep telling everyone every day, it's true so it's fine | Rip up their drawings | Only talk about yours
test: heard some kids got bored over summer n jumped off the quarry ledge, one broke both legs n an arm, my cousin knows them | Bored kids trying risky things get hurt, so pick a safe activity; the exact injuries may be blown up | Quarry jumping is completely safe as long as you are a strong swimmer and you jump feet first | It cannot be true, bored kids never take risks | It is fine as long as an older kid goes first
test: Finn keeps saying he's the best at everything n everyone's saying he's a show-off loser behind his back | Boasting pushes friends away, but calling him names behind his back is unkind too | Calling him a loser behind his back will teach him a good lesson, and he deserves it | Everyone should ignore him forever from now on | He is right to boast if he really is the best
test: my sister says if ur too excited to sleep before ur birthday u can't sleep at all n the day gets cancelled lol | That is not true; reading and slow breathing help, and sleeping makes the day come quicker | It is true, so the safest thing is to stay up all night and not even try to sleep before it | It is true, but only for birthdays, not holidays | It is true unless you drink a fizzy drink
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises boredom? | Boredom is frequently the starting point of creativity | Boredom is a sign of an illness which requires medicine | Boredom can be cured only by television screens and video games | Boredom is never experienced by truly intelligent children
exam: Identify the principal danger associated with a state of great excitement. | It becomes easy to cease noticing dangers such as wet floors, roads and water | It causes the body to become far too heavy to move for a period of several hours afterwards | It makes a person unable to hear any sound at all for the rest of the day | It invariably leads to illness unless the person sleeps for a whole day
exam: In respect of pride in one's own effort, select the response consistent with the established facts. | Pride in one's effort encourages continued improvement, whereas boasting pushes friends away | Pride in one's effort should always be concealed, since every expression of it amounts to boasting | Pride in one's effort is only permitted to those who have won a first prize in a contest | Pride in one's effort is best expressed by stating that one is superior to all others
exam: Pursuant to a lesson which a pupil finds too easy, which course of action is appropriate? | The pupil politely asks the teacher for a harder challenge | The pupil disrupts the class until a harder task is set | The pupil ceases to work entirely and simply waits for the lesson to end | The pupil informs the class that the lesson is pointless
